2. Modifications (share-alike)
------------------------------

The bundled file is NOT the original CC-CEDICT export. It is a modified version,
which matters because CC BY-SA 4.0 requires that modifications be shared under
the same licence. What was changed:

  - Stored as SQLite with a purpose-built schema (`words`,
    `dictionary_metadata`, `localized_definition_quality`) rather than the
    upstream line-based text format.
  - Each definition was translated into further languages, held in
    `words.definition_fr`, `definition_de`, `definition_es`, `definition_ru`,
    `definition_it`, `definition_pt`, `definition_ja`, `definition_ko`,
    `definition_vi`, `definition_id`, `definition_ar`, `definition_hi`,
    `definition_th` (and the English source in `definition`).
  - A per-definition quality score was computed and stored in
    `localized_definition_quality` (1,369,632 rows), keyed to the hash of the
    English source definition it was measured against.
  - Entries were filtered and rearranged; a content version is recorded as
    `dictionary_content_version = cc-cedict-localized-v1`.

OPEN QUESTION — not yet decided, and deliberately not asserted here:
whether the translated definitions above are "modifications" reached by the
share-alike condition (in which case they must themselves be offered under
CC BY-SA 4.0), or a separate work built on top of CC-CEDICT-derived text. The
attribution above is correct either way and is now complete; the share-alike
determination is tracked in ISSUES.md and is a question for a lawyer, because it
decides whether the translated dictionary can stay proprietary.
